welcome to the Macmillan report I'm  Marilyn Wilkes your host and today our  guest is Professor kalyana Krishna Shiva  Ramakrishna a professor in both the  anthropology and forestry and  environmental studies departments at  Yale as well as chair of the South Asian  Studies Council professor Shiva  Ramakrishna's research interests span  environmental history political  anthropology cultural geography  development and science studies he has  published widely in all of these fields  with a regional focus on South Asia  today we will be talking about his new  work on environmental conflicts in India  welcome professor Shiva Ramakrishna  thank you for having me here how did you  become interested in forests and  agricultural issues in India that goes  back a while because this is not my  first career I started out right out of  college going to work for the government  of India and in that job I worked first  extensively in eastern India as a rural  development officer getting a lot of  experience doing rural development  projects mainly due with agricultural  development issues and later I worked  for some years in the Government of  India the Ministry of Environment and  Forests planning an exciting new program  on the development of waste lands and  forest conservation programs that the  India was getting very interested in  doing the ministry was new at the time  and they were really expanding their  activities in natural resources  management and forest conservation so  that experience gave me a lot of  exposure to these topics and developed  my interest in these topics and I  realized what I really wanted to be  doing was studying them and teaching  about them so I ended up going to  graduate school and wrote my  dissertation on forest management and  conservation  in eastern India and the project started  out being primarily interested in  efforts to involve local villagers and  people who live in around for us in the  care of the forest that they depended on  but we also were valuable natural and  national resource and these projects had  been funded by various organizations  like the World Bank and the Ford  Foundation and the government also but  studying these fairly novel attempts to  get cooperation going between the  conservation agencies and local people I  realized I really should be looking at a  much longer history of forest management  in the region which took me into my  research into the environmental history  of the area and the life and cultures of  various tribal and other agricultural  communities in the area and the  historical research took me back almost  400 years into the beginnings of the  colonial period and I ended up working  on that topic for almost 10 years for my  dissertation and Beyond and wrote  several books and articles on that topic  and that that was sort of how I got into  this ok so let's talk about your newest  project tell us a little bit about it my  since then my research moved into  related areas I got interested in more  broadly in human animal relations  wildlife conservation and the  relationship between environmental  change and agriculture especially in  certain dry areas where there was  chronic food and livelihood insecurity  and how that might be related to the  scarcity of water and the poor  conditions in which people farmed and  studying that took me also to new  regions I went after having worked for  more than a decade in eastern India in  Bengal and in Assam I now moved my field  research to south India to the state of  Thurman art which is on the southernmost  part in there of India in just across  the sea from  lanka and working there I began to study  initially dryland farming but got  interested in issues of migration and  related issues of wide-ranging conflicts  around Natural Resources and scarcity of  Natural Resources let's talk a little  bit about specifically about those  conflicts what are you finding  well I found that in any particular area  the conflicts related to a number of  resources simultaneously because in  these areas people dependent both on  farming and and forests and on livestock  and animal herding and so on the  conflicts simultaneously hinged on  terrestrial resources as well as various  agricultural commodities and of course  labor this despite the fact that these  are fairly well populated regions  there's always endemic sort of seasonal  scarcity of Labor which leads to a lot  of migration and then conflicts go  beyond the local area into neighboring  areas as well the escalation of  agricultural crises both there and other  parts of the country led to increasing  levels and intensity of conflict and one  of the things that I noticed was that  more and more cases were being filed  there is there was more and more  litigation taking place around these  conflicts and there was from about the  mid-1980s a spate of cases leading to  going all the way up to the Supreme  Court leading to a number of rulings  that the Supreme Court handed down and I  realized that there was a whole new  domain that was emerging as a result of  environmental conflicts in which the  courts were getting very heavily  involved and basically creating new  conditions for the management and new  understandings of what our citizenship  rights what our environmental qualities  what kind of quality of life especially  clean air and clean water and adequate  access to green amenities people might  have  as citizens so I began to get much more  interested in the role of the courts in  these environmental conflicts okay and  why is it important to look at these  environmental kinds of issues well I  believe these design extremely important  issues I mean today the world is facing  a number of very challenging  environmental problems cause we know  best the ones relating to global climate  change because they've been made so  prominent thanks to the tireless efforts  of the IPCC and former Vice President Al  Gore and their efforts even won them a  Nobel Prize so everyone knows about  climate change but besides climate  change there are serious problems  relating to the productivity of  Agriculture and most of the world the  majority of farming still takes place in  dry conditions reliant on seasonal  availability of moisture through  monsoons and other weather events and  such agricultural systems are in grave  crisis which is why we are having what  appears to be a growing growing problem  of food crises for instance in the news  there have been a lot of talk recently  of an impending food crisis in various  parts of the world some of it has been  attributed to some countries like the  u.s. growing biofuels but nevertheless  what you begin to realize is there are  conflicts relating to climate relation  to agriculture and of course the the  disappearance of wide variety of plants  and animals and that the loss of  biodiversity and and related issues of  water and air pollution and toxicity in  soils and so on so there are a lot of  problems and many of them are manifest  on a large scale in a large country like  India where all its star always  terrestrial resources and quite a bit of  its atmospheric resources have been  heavily used and managed for several  hundred years at this point the other  reason why I think India is a  particularly useful  example through which to study some of  these issues is its rapid economic  growth in the last 10-15 years India has  been growing at a remarkably fast pace  and like China this poses along with  China this poses major environmental  questions that have to be addressed  urgently and I think studying them in a  place like India is particularly  exciting and rewarding because it is an  open society it's a democracy no  conflict can be easily resolved because  so many groups are involved so many  constituencies have to be dealt with and  all this has to be done in a way that is  as far as possible participatory and  democratic so in terms of your newest  project what are you hoping to find or  learn through your research i'm as i  said i'm interested particularly in the  emergence of this expanded judicial  intervention in this attempts to manage  and resolve environmental conflicts in  india so what i'm hoping to find is the  new patterns and systems of  environmental management of government  that are emerging through a combination  of the efforts of the courts the  executive branch of government the  democratic aspects that are the  legislative branches of government and  the vigorous involvement and activity of  civil society organizations ngos  activists of various kind and the  relationship all this has to the  aspirations and struggles of small-scale  local communities and growing and  extremely prosperous large middle  classes in the big cities and how these  might be matched how this might be  reconciled and how they might be managed  in a way that conflicts are mitigated  and in but their environment is also  protected and nurtured okay so what is  your next step with this project well  the next step is as soon as I can get  some leaves to go off and do some more  fieldwork and looking forward to doing  that in a short while in a year or two  okay wonderful well thank you so much  for coming and sharing some of your work  with us today for more information about  professor shiva ramakrishna and his work  please visit our website at yale.edu  backslash Macmillan report be sure to  join us again for another episode of the  Macmillan report made possible through  funding from the Whitney and Betty  Macmillan Center for International and  area studies at Yale thank you very much  thank you
 

